<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<PackageManifest Version="2.0.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema/2011"
                xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema-design/2011">
  <Metadata>
    <Identity Id="DeepSeekHarness.VisualStudio" Version="0.1.0" Language="en-US" Publisher="DeepSeekHarness" />
    <DisplayName>DeepSeek Harness</DisplayName>
    <Description xml:space="preserve">Review DeepSeek Harness file edits in Visual Studio's native diff window before they touch disk.

DeepSeek Harness (dsh) is a terminal agent. This extension gives it the one thing a terminal cannot: a real diff. When the agent wants to change a file, the diff opens beside your code &#8212; left is what is on disk, right is what the model proposes &#8212; and a small prompt offers Accept or Reject. Nothing is written until you accept, and a rejection can carry an explanation that goes straight back to the model.

Setup is just the VSIX. The extension writes the gate plugin DeepSeek Harness loads and advertises itself through a loopback lock file. No YAML editing, no scripts to copy into your repository, and nothing is added to your DeepSeek Harness profile.

Uninstalling removes the extension itself. Use "Tools &gt; DeepSeek Harness &gt; Remove everything" first: a VSIX uninstall cannot run code, so it would otherwise leave the plugin behind.</Description>
    <MoreInfo>https://github.com/</MoreInfo>
    <License>LICENSE.txt</License>
    <Tags>deepseek, dsh, agent, ai, diff, llm, coding assistant</Tags>
  </Metadata>
  <Installation>
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Community" Version="[17.14,19.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
  </Installation>
  <Dependencies>
    <Dependency Id="Microsoft.Framework.NDP" DisplayName="Microsoft .NET Framework" d:Source="Manual" Version="[4.7.2,)" />
  </Dependencies>
  <Prerequisites>
    <Prerequisite Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.CoreEditor" Version="[17.14,19.0)" DisplayName="Visual Studio core editor" />
  </Prerequisites>
  <Assets>
    <!-- PkgdefProjectOutputGroup is what the VSSDK generates from the package's
         [Guid] and registration attributes; the template uses the same asset. -->
    <Asset Type="Microsoft.VisualStudio.VsPackage" d:Source="File" Path="DeepSeekHarness.pkgdef" />
    <Asset Type="Microsoft.VisualStudio.MefComponent" d:Source="File" Path="DeepSeekHarness.VS.dll" />
  </Assets>
</PackageManifest>
